[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"932","stub":{"id":"932","section":"3) Milltown to Calais","altname":"Skutik","river":"St. Croix ","difficulty":"IItoV","states":["CAN-NBR","USA-MAI"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"250","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null},"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":"The put in is to the left of the Border Patrol station. Park in the small dirt lot to the right (before the train tracks) and walk over. It is very common for a concerned Border guard to come out and ask what exactly you are doing, but they have never tried to stop me.","id":"108931","location":{"latitude":"45.1693","longitude":"-67.2966"},"name":"Put In","distance":"0","photoID":null,"difficulty":"III","approximate":false},{"type":"rapid","description":"The first rapid of this reach is made up of 3 channels. Scouting from land is almost impossible, especially due to the US/Canada border dividing the river. Line up for the channel you want at the bridge, as crossing between them quickly becomes difficult. The farthest left, and the center channel, are class 4+ and appear to need at least 2,000 cfs to run at all. Both are short and steep with a decent final drop. River right channel is flatter and longer, and quite bony below 1,700 cfs, but runnable. Between 1,700-2,200 this channel is probably a stout class 3, but quickly moves to class 4 around 2,200 cfs.\n\nAt higher flows, the center channel appears to have dangerous holes. The river right channel is powerful class 3+ to 4 at 9,000 cfs.\n\nALL CHANNELS CONTAIN OLD DAM PILINGS AND METAL - SWIM CAREFULLY.","id":"m6ZLxepuynZJwfZSOBKA1","location":{"latitude":"45.175004","longitude":"-67.292123"},"name":"Milltown Rapid","distance":"0.57","difficulty":"IV","approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"rapid","description":"This site was formerly known as Salmon Falls before construction of the Milltown Dam (removed 2023-2024, restoring the river and fish passage). There is a park on the New Brunswick side. On the US side, there is a view from the shoulder of Rt 1 across from The Sandwich Man shop, or it can be scouted from the railroad tracks below Poorhouse Rd.","id":"jonONaE5oiUICUsMEzuhn","location":{"latitude":"45.175456","longitude":"-67.292712"},"name":"Salmon Falls (Former Site of Milltown Dam)","distance":"0.63","difficulty":"III+","approximate":false,"photoID":"PbKn3CeWa8n0CG6dDG1pY"},{"type":"portage","description":"About a mile of class 1-2 water depending on level","id":"jjqRreXhm2XURa24Ey2q3","location":{"latitude":"45.180704","longitude":"-67.291552"},"name":"Middle Rips","distance":"1.04","photoID":null,"difficulty":"II","approximate":false},{"type":"portage","description":"Short tidal rip, class 3-4 depending on river level and time of tide. Only appears on the lower end of the tide, so it is best hit from about 3 hours before low tide to about 3 hours after. From here paddle to the silver floating dock at Pike's Park in Calais (US side)","id":"uImb7sHPu6TknvdjFNyL9","location":{"latitude":"45.189532","longitude":"-67.286828"},"name":"Calais Tidal Rapid","distance":"1.82","photoID":null,"difficulty":"III","approximate":false},{"type":"takeout","description":"https://maps.app.goo.gl/DZWYeaPCkf9opjAY9?g\\_st=com.google.maps.preview.copy","id":"114168","location":{"latitude":"45.190304","longitude":"-67.277068"},"name":"Take Out - Calais Waterfront Boat Landing","distance":"2.4","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[{"id":"WOr40EKBLPdyySMbas2fQ","title":null,"post_date":"2021-09-09 00:13:17+00","detail":"The water flow readings for this section are incorrect. The gauge at Baring has the correct flow data for this stretch, and can be found on the USGS page.","user":{"name":"Nate G"}}],"photos":[{"id":"Ei01Oklei8UluAwE2wGnO","ext":"jpg","post_id":"1ikFsFpW6LO7IYZuBB3Re","description":"below the eddy below the railroad bridge","caption":"Biggest feature","poi_name":null,"subject":"At 9,000 cfs - crux of the rapid","author":"Mark Berry","poi_id":"jonONaE5oiUICUsMEzuhn","photo_date":"2025-04-19T11:21:08.000Z","updated_at":"2025-04-20T11:21:08.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/Ei01Oklei8UluAwE2wGnO","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/Ei01Oklei8UluAwE2wGnO"}},{"id":"PbKn3CeWa8n0CG6dDG1pY","ext":"jpg","post_id":"1ikFsFpW6LO7IYZuBB3Re","description":"Eddy on river right provides good line for wave below. ","caption":"At 9,000 cfs ","poi_name":null,"subject":"Immediately below railroad bridge.","author":"Mark Berry","poi_id":"jonONaE5oiUICUsMEzuhn","photo_date":"2025-04-19T11:21:08.000Z","updated_at":"2025-04-20T11:21:08.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/PbKn3CeWa8n0CG6dDG1pY","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/PbKn3CeWa8n0CG6dDG1pY"}}],"detail":{"id":"932","permitUrl":null,"permitInfo":null,"description":"See also [Vanceboro to Milltown](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/content/River/view/river-detail/905).\n\nThe 2023-2024 removal of the Milltown dam changed this from a short run with an inconvenient portage to a stretch of quality whitewater. Paddlers should scout the former dam site carefully, and be aware of powerful hydraulics in Milltown rapids at higher flows.\n\nLat/ longitude coordinates are a **SWAG**.\n\nPut in to the left of the two buildings at the border crossing on North St (US side of border).","averageGradient":null,"maxGradient":null,"length":"2.4","correlations":[{"reachID":"932","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"700","beginMediumRunnable":"1500","beginHighRunn